-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/ed340f8b-ff12-529b-8434-d73f0c84bc55
-- title:
--   Drinfeld's standard special formal O_D-module, and a box basis lemma
-- statement:
--   Two bodies of material. First, an appendix on monomial ideals: for a commutative ring $R$ and $N : \mathrm{Fin}\,g \to \mathbb N$, `powIdeal` is the ideal of $R[[X_0,\dots,X_{g-1}]]$ generated by the $X_i^{N_i}$, `InBox N d` says $d_i < N_i$ for all $i$, and `boxKer` is the ideal of series all of whose coefficients inside that box vanish. The two ideals coincide (`powIdeal_eq_boxKer`), proved by writing any series in `boxKer` as $\sum_i X_i^{N_i}\cdot$`part`$_i$, where `part` redistributes coefficients according to the first coordinate leaving the box; `extend` conversely turns a function on $\prod_i \mathrm{Fin}(N_i)$ into a series supported in the box, giving an explicit $R$-linear isomorphism `boxEquiv` from $\big(\prod_i \mathrm{Fin}(N_i)\to R\big)$ onto the quotient. The packaged theorem states, for nontrivial $R$, that the quotient is free and finite over $R$ of rank $\prod_i N_i$.
--
--   Second, Drinfeld's standard formal module over $\mathcal O =$ `Zp2 p` $= W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$, with $q$ the cardinality of $\mathbb F_{p^2}$. Each defining series is produced by the Lubin–Tate construction `lt` from its linear part and pinned down by the uniqueness principle `eq_of_comm`: `lawSeries` from the linear form sending the two $i$-th variables to $1$; `actSeries a` from the form with diagonal entry $\chi_i(a)$, where $\chi_0 = \mathrm{id}$ and $\chi_1 =$ Witt-vector Frobenius; `varpiSeries` from the forms $p\,X_1$ and $X_0$. Thus `law` is the two-dimensional group law $(\Sigma(X_0,Y_0),\Sigma(X_1,Y_1))$, commutative, $a$ acts by $([a],[\sigma a])$ and $\varpi$ by $([p](X_1),X_0)$; `standard` assembles these into a `FormalODModule p 𝕆`, with `standard_act_p` identifying the action of $p$ with the Lubin–Tate series $pT + T^{q}$ in each variable.
--
--   For a commutative ring $B$ of characteristic $p$ and $j : \mathcal O \to B$, `modB j` is the base change. Its tangent action is diagonal, $a\cdot m = (j(\chi_i a)m_i)_i$; hence `lieZero j` and `lieOne j` are the two coordinate axes (using that $j(\sigma a_0 - a_0)$ is a unit for suitable $a_0$), which are complementary and each free of rank one, giving `isSpecial`. Over $B$ the action of $p$ becomes $(X_i^{q})$, whose kernel is finite, projective and of fibrewise rank $p^4$ by the box lemma, giving `hasHeight 4`; `SpecialFormalODModule.standard j` bundles these.
--
--   Finally the biproduct structure: `incl i`, `proj i` exhibit `(modB j).F` as a product of two copies of `sigmaBar j`, with matrix units `cell i i' e` $=$ `incl i` $\circ\, e\, \circ$ `proj i'` and entries `entry i i' E`, satisfying the usual multiplication table, additivity, $1 = e_{00}+e_{11}$, the decomposition of an arbitrary endomorphism into its four entries, and the identities `actEnd_eq` ($a \mapsto \mathrm{diag}(\rho(a),\rho(\sigma a))$) and `varpiEnd_eq` ($\varpi \mapsto$ the antidiagonal matrix with entries $\rho(p)$ and $1$). Auxiliary lemmas on associativity and unit laws for composition of homomorphisms of multivariate formal group laws are also recorded.
--
--   **Relation to Mathlib.** Mathlib has no multivariate formal group laws nor formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules; those notions are the project's own, built on Mathlib's `MvPowerSeries` substitution calculus. The box basis lemma is a statement about Mathlib's `MvPowerSeries` quotients by monomial ideals.
--
--   **Where it is used.** The standard special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module of height four is the base point of Drinfeld's moduli problem of special formal modules, whose rigidified deformations give the Čerednik–Drinfeld $p$-adic uniformisation of Shimura curves; this uniformisation underlies the study of the bad-reduction fibres used in level lowering.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Definitions/Def_CerednikDrinfeld_StandardFormalODModule.lean

